{\cf2\b\fs34 GRANULAR - Help\par}
{\cf5\i FM \'b7 Synthesis \'b7 HABI M \'b7 Version 2.7\par}\par
{\cf5\fs16 GranularSynth \'a9 2026 Matthias Haberecht \'b7 free software under the GNU GPL version 3 or later, without warranty \'b7 licence text: LICENSE.txt in the plugin folder\par}\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 Foreword\par}
GranularSynth is a highly optimised granular synthesizer. The CPU-heavy parts (grains, oscillator, reverb, equalizer, displays) are written in {\b assembler} (SSE, Win32 and Win64), each with a Pascal version for cross-checking. The result: {\b very low CPU load} with {\b high sound quality} - even on older computers and in old hosts. The interface language can be switched between Deutsch and English in the Extras tab ("Sprache / Language") or by clicking the small flag at the right end of the tab row.\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 1. What is granular synthesis?\par}
A sound (a WAV file) is chopped into many tiny pieces called {\b grains} (usually 20 to 300 ms long). These are played back over and over, layered, shifted and re-pitched. That way a short recording turns into pads, clouds, rhythms or completely new instruments.\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 2. Getting started\par}
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Choose a preset:} in the list at the top, step through with < and >.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Play:} on-screen keyboard at the bottom, MIDI keyboard or computer keyboard (A W S E D F ...).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Your own sound:} "Load audio..." - any WAV/MP3 becomes raw material.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Quick tab:} macros (Texture, Motion, Space ...) change many things at once; 50 % = preset unchanged.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Info:} describes live, in plain words, what happens when you press a key.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Pre-roll} (Grain tab, below the envelope): the note starts at the {\b pre-roll start} before the selection, plays through once into the selection and then works there as usual - e.g. the attack or a breath first, then the floating loop. Easiest in the waveform: drag the {\b yellow line} (right-click, or grab it with the left button) in front of the white selection edge - the note starts there (e.g. at the attack), runs into the selection and afterwards repeats only the marked range. If the yellow line is inside the selection, pre-roll is off and the line is the position as before. {\b Ping-pong loop}: the scan turns around at the end of the selection instead of jumping back to the start (smoother, no jump).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Drag in audio files:} drop a file from Explorer onto the window = new main sample.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Samples tab - zones:} up to three more samples, each from a start key upwards (e.g. cello low, violin high). Files dropped onto a zone row land in that zone.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Samples tab - recording:} records the sidechain input (up to 60 s) and loads it as the main sample - granulate the DAW track or your own voice live. The recording is then kept as a WAV in the "Eigene Toene" (own sounds) folder.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Render in highest quality} (Performance tab): DAW exports always use HQ and the full voice count, even if live playback is set to save CPU.\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 3. The signal path\par}
Key {\cf2\b \'bb} Arpeggiator {\cf2\b \'bb} voice with envelope {\cf2\b \'bb} grains from the sample + oscillator {\cf2\b \'bb} Filter 1/2 {\cf2\b \'bb} Effects {\cf2\b \'bb} Echo {\cf2\b \'bb} Reverb {\cf2\b \'bb} Saturation {\cf2\b \'bb} Equalizer {\cf2\b \'bb} Output.\par\par{\b Per-note sound engine:} every note you play has its own grains, its own oscillator, its own Filter 1 and 2 with its own filter envelope. Sources tied to a note (velocity, random per note, note pitch, envelopes, LFOs retriggered on "note on/off") only affect their own note - one note can pitch away or open up while the others stay put. Echo, reverb, EQ and limiter then act on all notes together.
Every control has a {\b base value}. Modulation shifts it temporarily - the red line under a control shows the current effective value.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Filter types} (Filter 1 and 2): low-pass, band-pass, high-pass, {\b Ladder 24 dB} (warm and slightly driven, steep like a Moog), {\b Comb} (metallic, cutoff = pitch of the comb, resonance = feedback) and {\b Formant} (vowel filter: the cutoff glides smoothly from A through E, I, O to U - "talking" sounds via LFO).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Effects tab:} chorus/flanger, phaser, bitcrusher and {\b trance gate} (16 clickable steps, in song tempo). They sit on the sum after the filters and before echo and reverb.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Reverb:} Bass cut and Treble cut filter only the reverb signal - the dry sound stays untouched (like the reverb in FL Studio).\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 4. Modulation: sources, matrix, targets\par}
The heart of the synth is the {\b modulation matrix} ("Modulation" tab) with {\b 32 rows} on four pages - the buttons at the top right show in brackets how many rows are used on each page. "Modulate with" in a control's right-click menu always takes the topmost free row. Each row connects a {\b Source} to a {\b Target}:\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Sources:} LFO 1-8, envelopes, velocity, mod wheel, aftertouch, pitch bend, random per note, sidechain level and pitch, threshold gate.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Targets:} almost every control - pitch, filter, position in the sample, echo, reverb, EQ bands, even the rate of other LFOs.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Amount, Factor, Offset:} how strongly, how far and in which direction the source acts.\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 5. LFOs and triggers\par}
An {\b LFO} is a slow oscillation (sine, triangle, saw, square, random or {\b free}-drawn). It can {\b run continuously} or be restarted by a {\b trigger}:\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Note on / Note off:} on every key press or release.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b End of partner LFO:} one LFO kicks off the next - this is how chains are built.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Sidechain trigger:} when the input signal is loud enough and/or high enough.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Threshold gate / Silence:} when any source crosses a threshold or stays quiet for a long time.\par
With {\b One-shot} the LFO runs through only once - ideal for melodies: draw the "Free" shape in semitones, "Assign to pitch", done.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Stop:} every LFO can be stopped by an event (note off, end of partner LFO, sidechain, threshold ...) or {\b after a time} - in milliseconds or in song tempo (sync).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Partner:} freely choose which LFO kicks off the next one - so chains can be built in any order.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Eight LFOs:} in the "LFO 1-8 / Trigger" tab the button at the bottom right switches between {\b LFO 1-4} and {\b LFO 5-8} (in brackets: how many of LFOs 5-8 are in use). All eight can do the same and are matrix sources; an LFO 5-8 that nothing uses costs no CPU.\par

{\cf3\b\fs26 6. Oscillators and mixer\par}
In addition to the grains, up to {\b three oscillators} (tabs Osc 1, Osc 2, Osc 3) play along with every note: sine, triangle, saw, square, FM or noise - each with its own envelope, its own FM envelope and its own filter. Great for bass, bells, kicks or hi-hats. The {\b Sync} control turns a clean wave into an ever richer, cutting tone (0 = clean). With the {\b Grains on} checkbox (Grain tab) unticked only the oscillators play - GranularSynth then is a classic synthesizer, while filters, echo, reverb and matrix keep working. The {\b Mixer} tab shows all volumes side by side (grains, Osc 1-3, echo, reverb, sum, master) - these are the same controls as in the other tabs. The mixer also shows the {\b Inputs (DAW)} row with the levels E1-E4 and the {\b performance level}: it limits voices and grains if the computer runs short of power. The {\b Performance} tab shows which sound modules cost how much CPU (list and meter), plus the measured run time in the host.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Unison} (per oscillator): 2 to 7 voices per note, spread apart by the {\b detune} and across the stereo field by the {\b width} - the fat sound of modern pads and leads. Each voice costs some CPU.\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 7. Morph: blending two sounds\par}
A second preset is stored as {\b target B} ("Extras" tab). The {\b Morph} control blends seamlessly between A and B - by hand, with its own {\b morph LFO} (which can also be drawn freely) or via the matrix.\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 8. Playing aids\par}
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Arpeggiator} with step pattern and {\b scale} lock (never a wrong note).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Glide:} notes slide into each other, adjustable time.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Bend / Mod} next to the keyboard: bend the pitch (springs back) and mod wheel.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Latch} (arp): like on the microKORG the arpeggio keeps running after you let go; a new chord replaces the old one, turning Latch off ends it.\par\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 9. Sidechain\par}
A second signal (e.g. a microphone or a track in the host) can take control: its level ducks the sound, its pitch is followed (Auto-Pitch), triggers start LFOs. The learning presets {\b L13 to L23} show this step by step.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b FX variant:} "GranularSynth FX.dll" registers as an effect and can be put into a mixer insert slot; the dry signal then passes through unchanged and the synth sound is added.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b FL Studio:} put the FX variant into the mixer channel, choose a port under MIDI, create a MIDI Out channel with the same port and play the notes on it.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Inputs:} the Mixer tab sets which input pair (E1-E4) feeds sidechain 1 and 2; the level meters show whether a signal is arriving.\par
\par

{\cf3\b\fs26 10. Tips\par}
{\cf4\b \'95 }The learning presets {\b L01 to L34} each explain one feature - listen through them in order (file LERNPFAD.txt, the learning path).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }Your own presets: "Save..." - your own sounds are copied along (into the "Eigene Toene" (own sounds) folder).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }The Master control in the Quick tab is kept when you change presets.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Automation in the DAW:} every control movement is reported to the DAW - automation can be recorded, and in FL Studio {\b Tools > Last tweaked > Create automation clip} creates an automation clip for the last control you moved (also in the right-click menu: "Automation in the DAW" reports the control).\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b VST3:} GranularSynth.vst3 contains the instrument and the FX. In newer FL versions, right-clicking a control shows the FL menu directly (automation clip, link to controller) plus an entry for the GranularSynth menu. The banner shows after the version number which variant is running (VST2, VST3, each also as FX).\par

{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Undo / Redo:} Ctrl+Z undoes every control movement (including preset changes, Vary, A/B), Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes it.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Modulation on the control:} a turquoise bar in the track shows how far the matrix moves the control. {\b Alt + mouse wheel} on the control changes the amount in 5 % steps; the right-click menu shows which sources are acting on it.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b MIDI Learn:} right-click a control > MIDI Learn, then turn a knob on your controller - done. The assignment applies to all projects (file MidiLearn.txt in the plugin folder); Esc cancels, and it can be removed again in the same menu.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }{\b Finding presets:} choose a {\b category} to the left of the filter (Pads, Bass, Lead, Arp, Bells, Instruments, Voice, Nature/FX, Morph, Learning). The {\b star} marks the current preset as a favourite; the category \u9733? Favourites shows only those.\par
{\cf4\b \'95 }Saving CPU: lower the performance level, switch HQ off, use reverb/echo only where needed - the {\b Performance} tab shows what is expensive. During silence GranularSynth computes almost nothing.\par
\par
{\cf3\b\fs26 11. All controls at a glance\par}
What every control and button does - tab by tab. Column {\b VST parameters}: {\cf4 yes} = automatable in the host and a target in the modulation matrix, {\cf5 no} = only in the plugin window, {\cf5 MIDI} = comes as a MIDI signal. The tempo always comes from the host.\par\par
